('PSY-LF05-03','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',1,'attachment','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.1.3, p18 — Bowlby''s monotropic theory, critical period and internal working model','Attachment','Describe and evaluate Bowlby''s monotropic theory as an explanation of the formation and later influence of infant attachments.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain monotropy, social releasers, the proposed critical period and evolutionary/adaptive role.","2. Explain the internal working model as a proposed foundation for expectations of later relationships.","3. Distinguish Bowlby''s claims from learning-theory accounts where useful.","1. Evaluate relevant research on early attachment and later relationships, avoiding causal overclaiming.","2. Question the universality of monotropy and the evidence for a strict critical rather than sensitive period.","3. Consider alternative explanations, including continuity of care and later experiences.","4. Make a judgement balancing the theory''s explanatory reach against deterministic claims.","Alternative valid answer routes: Accept careful discussion of institutionalisation or early deprivation when causally qualified. Do not equate separation, deprivation and institutionalisation as the same construct.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-03; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-03: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-04','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',1,'clinical-psychology-and-mental-health','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.1.4, p18 — cognitive explanations and CBT treatment of depression','Clinical Psychology and Mental Health','Describe and evaluate cognitive explanations of depression and the use of cognitive behaviour therapy to treat depression.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain Beck''s negative triad and Ellis''s ABC model, including irrational beliefs.","2. Outline how CBT uses the challenge of unhelpful thoughts in treatment.","3. Distinguish an explanation of depression from evidence of treatment effectiveness.","1. Evaluate supportive treatment evidence while distinguishing effectiveness from proof of cause.","2. Discuss feasibility, engagement, relapse and whether cognitive interventions suit all presentations.","3. Consider the direction of causality between negative thinking and symptoms, and relevant alternatives.","4. Develop a qualified judgement about explanatory usefulness and treatment appropriateness.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit evidence-informed treatment comparisons if relevant, without inventing effect sizes. Avoid outdated 2027 content outside the specified Beck/Ellis/CBT requirements.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-04; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-04: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-05','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',2,'approaches-in-psychology','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.2.1, p19 — behaviourist approach and social learning theory; comparison of approaches','Approaches in Psychology','Compare the behaviourist approach with social learning theory as explanations of how behaviour is acquired. Evaluate the value of each approach.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Accurately explain classical conditioning, operant conditioning and reinforcement in behaviourism.","2. Explain imitation, identification, vicarious reinforcement and mediational processes in social learning theory.","3. Identify a meaningful distinction between direct reinforcement and observed learning.","1. Use relevant research (such as Pavlov, Skinner or Bandura) to evaluate scientific control and ecological validity.","2. Compare cognitive mediation in social learning theory with the relative emphasis on observable behaviour in behaviourism.","3. Evaluate determinism, explanatory scope and individual/cultural differences without treating labels as substitutes for reasoning.","4. Reach an argued comparative conclusion.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit alternative sound comparison of predictions, methods and practical applications. Avoid crediting inaccurate assertion that social learning theory rejects reinforcement.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-05; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-05: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-06','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',2,'biopsychology','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.2.2, p19 — ways of studying the brain; localisation/plasticity','Biopsychology','Discuss the usefulness and limitations of fMRI, EEG and post-mortem examinations for investigating localisation of brain function.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Identify the kind of evidence each technique provides: fMRI blood-oxygen-related activity, EEG electrical activity and examination of brain tissue.","2. Distinguish temporal and spatial information and the nature of evidence available from each method.","3. Connect these techniques to investigating localisation of function without presenting correlation as localisation proof.","1. Compare spatial versus temporal resolution and the inferential limits of fMRI.","2. Evaluate EEG''s temporal strengths and difficulties identifying precise deep locations.","3. Evaluate post-mortem structural insights alongside retrospective, confounding and causal limitations.","4. Draw a justified conclusion about combining methods for converging evidence.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit well-linked discussion of ERPs and split-brain evidence when relevant. Do not ask learners to evaluate the endocrine system as if it were a theory; this question targets methods.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-06; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-06: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-08','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'issues-and-debates-in-psychology','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.1, pp21–22 — nature–nurture debate and interactionist approach','Issues and debates in Psychology','Discuss the nature–nurture debate in psychology, using relevant examples from other topics of psychology you have studied.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain the distinction between heritable/biological and environmental influences.","2. Define interactionism rather than portraying heredity and environment as mutually exclusive.","3. Accurately select relevant examples from compulsory topics that students have studied; examples from an optional topic are also creditworthy if the learner has studied that option.","1. Evaluate explanatory limits of strictly nature-only or nurture-only accounts using connected psychological evidence.","2. Discuss how gene–environment interplay changes interpretation and practical intervention.","3. Evaluate scientific/ethical implications of deterministic oversimplification where pertinent.","4. Build a synoptic judgement grounded in appropriately selected studied topics and the quality of relevant evidence, rather than rewarding a fixed number of topic names.","Alternative valid answer routes: First illustrate valid links using common compulsory content, such as attachment, biopsychology, memory or approaches. Also credit sound examples from aggression, schizophrenia or any other relevant optional content where the learner has actually studied that option. No fixed named-study list or minimum topic count is an AQA-wide rule; award credit for accurate explicit links, developed evaluation and defensible alternatives.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-08; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-08: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-09','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'relationships','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.2, p22 — social exchange theory, equity theory, investment model','Relationships','Describe and evaluate explanations of why people remain in romantic relationships, referring to social exchange theory and Rusbult''s investment model.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain perceived rewards, costs and comparison levels in social exchange theory.","2. Describe satisfaction, alternatives, investment and commitment in the investment model.","3. Distinguish staying in a relationship from judging its short-term quality.","1. Evaluate supporting research and methodological limits of retrospective/self-report relationship measures.","2. Compare explanatory coverage of long-term commitment with short-term cost–reward calculation.","3. Discuss social/cultural and individual differences and whether rational calculations reflect actual relationship decisions.","4. Offer a balanced comparison tied to persistence in relationships.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit appropriately used equity theory as comparative evaluation. Avoid mixing concepts of Duck''s breakdown phases into a demand solely about maintenance without a reason.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-09; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-09: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-10','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'gender','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.3, p22 — cognitive explanations Kohlberg and gender schema theory','Gender','Discuss cognitive explanations of gender development, including Kohlberg''s theory and Martin and Halverson''s gender schema theory.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Accurately describe gender identity, stability and constancy in Kohlberg''s account.","2. Explain schema-based information processing and attention to gender-related information in gender schema theory.","3. Distinguish developmental stages from schema-based processes.","1. Consider relevant cognitive development evidence and how it supports or challenges age-related predictions.","2. Assess whether schemas explain variation and change, and how social learning/culture influences construction of schemas.","3. Evaluate methodological problems in measuring complex gender concepts sensitively and accurately.","4. Develop a careful judgement that does not assume all identities develop identically.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit specification-consistent discussion of non-binary and gender-fluid identities as evaluation of assumptions. Avoid obsolete stereotypes, causal overclaims and treating chromosomal sex as identical to gender identity.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-10; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-10: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-11','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'cognition-and-development','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.4, p22 — Piaget and Vygotsky; learning and development','Cognition and development','Compare and evaluate Piaget''s and Vygotsky''s explanations of cognitive development, including their implications for how children learn.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain schemas, assimilation, accommodation and equilibration in Piaget''s account.","2. Explain the zone of proximal development and scaffolding in Vygotsky''s account.","3. Contrast independent maturation/discovery with socially supported learning without caricaturing either view.","1. Evaluate research evidence on stages and task demands, including alternative interpretations of children''s performance.","2. Discuss the role of language, social interaction and culture as a strength or boundary of each theory.","3. Assess educational implications with qualified claims and methodological safeguards.","4. Make a reasoned comparative evaluation of what each account explains well and less well about cognitive development and classroom learning, with evidence-sensitive qualifications rather than a list of names.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit knowledge of object permanence or conservation when explicitly relevant. Avoid attributing all cognitive change only to age or only to instruction.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-11; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-11: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-12','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'schizophrenia','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.5, pp22–23 — diathesis–stress and interactionist explanation/treatment','Schizophrenia','Discuss the interactionist approach to explaining and treating schizophrenia, including the diathesis–stress model.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Define vulnerability/diathesis and stress and show how they can interact.","2. Describe relevant biological and psychological influences on presentation.","3. Explain implications for combining drug and psychological therapies.","1. Evaluate evidence for combined vulnerability-and-stress explanations rather than independent single causes.","2. Consider merits and limits of treatment combinations and individual variation in response.","3. Address methodological and ethical problems including heterogeneity of symptoms and difficulties inferring causation.","4. Reach a qualified conclusion about explanatory and clinical usefulness.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit evidence about dopamine/genetic correlates or dysfunctional thinking when coherently linked. Avoid implying patients or families are personally responsible for illness.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-12; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-12: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-13','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'eating-behaviour','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.6, p23 — biological and psychological explanations of anorexia nervosa','Eating behaviour','Discuss biological and psychological explanations for anorexia nervosa, evaluating how well they explain its development.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain genetic/neural vulnerability at an appropriate conceptual level.","2. Describe at least one specified psychological explanation, such as family systems, social learning or cognitive distortions.","3. Distinguish a proposed cause from a consequence or correlate of disordered eating.","1. Evaluate relevant family, twin or neurobiological evidence with confounding cautions.","2. Consider cultural, media and individual differences that challenge single-factor accounts.","3. Assess bidirectional influences and limitations of retrospective or clinical samples.","4. Make an interactionist conclusion with careful attention to sensitivity.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit well-supported comparison of family systems, social learning and cognitive approaches. Avoid implying a particular family pattern inevitably causes anorexia.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-13; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-13: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-14','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'stress','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.7, p23 — stress inoculation therapy and biofeedback; managing stress','Stress','Describe and evaluate stress inoculation therapy and biofeedback as ways of helping people manage stress.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain the phases and coping-skills emphasis of stress inoculation therapy.","2. Explain biofeedback as monitoring physiological signals to learn control strategies.","3. Distinguish psychological coping training from physiological feedback mechanisms.","1. Evaluate evidence of effectiveness, considering outcome measures and durability.","2. Compare individual suitability, access, cost and skill maintenance rather than assuming equal benefit.","3. Consider broader stressors (such as workload or lack of control) that individual treatments may not remove.","4. Reach an argued conditional recommendation or comparative conclusion.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit relevant discussion of social support or workplace stressors as evaluation. Avoid implying that biofeedback removes the original stressor or that one therapy always works.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-14; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-14: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-15','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'aggression','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.8, p23 — frustration–aggression, social learning and de-individuation','Aggression','Discuss social psychological explanations of human aggression, referring to the frustration–aggression hypothesis and social learning theory.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain the proposed link between frustration and aggressive responses, including when it may not occur.","2. Explain imitation, identification, reinforcement and mediated processes in social learning.","3. Distinguish explanations based on immediate frustration from learned behavioural patterns.","1. Evaluate the strength and limitations of supporting observational and experimental evidence.","2. Discuss why frustration need not lead to aggression and the roles of appraisal or alternative responses.","3. Compare cultural/contextual predictions and methodological/ethical limits.","4. Conclude on interaction with biological or situational influences.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit de-individuation research as comparison if directly relevant. Do not claim media exposure inevitably causes violent behaviour.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-15; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-15: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'forensic-psychology','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.9, pp23–24 — offender profiling, typology, investigative and geographical profiling','Forensic Psychology','Compare typology-based and data-driven offender profiling, evaluating their usefulness to criminal investigations.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain organised/disorganised categorisation in typology profiling.","2. Explain data-driven investigative psychology as an offender-profiling approach using patterns in case data; distinguish it from geographical profiling, which considers patterns in offence locations. Both appear in 7182 §4.3.9 but are not synonyms.","3. Identify the types of information available to investigators and distinguish hypotheses from proof.","1. Evaluate empirical validity, consistency and predictive usefulness of types.","2. Assess strengths and biases of data-driven patterns, including sample representativeness and false positives.","3. Discuss ethical and practical risks when profiling directs limited resources.","4. Develop a conditional judgement about investigative support versus forensic proof.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit detailed evidence-based investigative psychology as the data-driven approach named in the question. Geographical profiling may receive relevant comparative credit when accurately identified as a distinct profiling approach, not substituted as a synonym for investigative psychology. Do not claim a profile proves guilt or uniquely identifies an offender.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-16; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-16: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-17','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',3,'addiction','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA Psychology 7182 v1.4 §4.3.10, p24 — gambling addiction, reinforcement and cognitive bias','Addiction','Discuss learning and cognitive explanations for gambling addiction, evaluating their relative usefulness.',NULL,16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","1. Explain partial/variable reinforcement and persistence of gambling behaviour.","2. Describe relevant cognitive biases in perceived probability or control.","3. Relate these to persistence despite negative consequences without treating all gambling as addiction.","1. Evaluate experimental/clinical evidence and limits of extrapolation to real gambling contexts.","2. Compare the explanatory power of reinforcement schedules with belief-based accounts.","3. Discuss vulnerability and situational factors that neither model explains completely.","4. Reach a balanced conclusion on the value of combining cognitive and behavioural approaches.","Alternative valid answer routes: Credit cautiously interpreted links to psychological or social risk factors. Avoid unsupported diagnostic claims or implying that all gamblers share the same cognitive biases.","Higher-band discrimination: A strong answer selects relevant accurate explanations, builds evidence-based counterargument and makes an explicit judgement responsive to this specific command. A weaker response describes terminology, recites generic evaluation or lists studies without explaining their bearing. Use the provisional level grid above and independent exemplars before assigning any purportedly calibrated mark."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-17; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-17: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-01-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'business','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.1','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','To what extent should Marshbrook Bakery prioritise cash flow rather than profit when deciding whether to expand?','Marshbrook Bakery Ltd has revenue of £1.8m, total costs of £1.74m and £90,000 of cash available. A chain is opening three nearby shops; managers want to borrow £400,000 to expand but several owners prefer to retain profits.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Differentiate accounting profit, cash flow and business objectives, including survival, growth and social objectives.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use the £60,000 profit (revenue minus total costs), £90,000 cash and proposed £400,000 borrowing; distinguish profitability from liquidity.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Explain how low cash buffers constrain finance, wages or suppliers despite reported profit. Develop how competition could change demand, pricing and fixed-cost exposure.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Weigh short-run cash resilience against profitable expansion and competitive positioning; conditional decision depends on repayment timing and forecast receipts.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-01-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-01-16: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-04-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'operations','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.4','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Assess whether Pine Components should prioritise improving quality rather than increasing capacity.','Pine Components plc operates at 96% capacity utilisation and has a 7% rework rate. Deliveries are late on 14% of orders. A new machine costs £1.5m but managers fear that reducing buffer stock will increase interruptions.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Explain quality, capacity utilisation, unit cost and the role of inventory and lean methods.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use 96% capacity, 7% rework and 14% late orders to identify quality and capacity pressures.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Show how fewer defects could free productive capacity and reduce costs, but also how the near-full plant constrains output. Analyse spending on automation and the effect of delays on reputation and long-run sales.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Prioritise the highest net cost of defects/delay, and condition the investment decision on verified throughput and demand forecasts.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-04-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-04-16: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-05-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'finance','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.5','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Evaluate whether a long-term bank loan is a suitable way for Harbour Furniture to finance its expansion.','Harbour Furniture Ltd reports operating profit of £480,000 on sales of £6m, current assets of £700,000 and current liabilities of £800,000. A £1m expansion could be financed by a secured loan at a variable rate, but orders are seasonal.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Distinguish internal/external finance, borrowing costs, liquidity and profitability measures.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Calculate or correctly interpret operating profit margin 8%, current ratio 0.875:1, and net working capital −£100,000 alongside the £1m funding requirement and seasonal receipts. The ratio may indicate liquidity pressure; it is not proof of insolvency.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Analyse finance access and ownership retention against interest, collateral and cash-outflow risk. Compare retained profit, equity or leasing with scale and timing of required expenditure.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Credit a defensible loan-or-alternative decision conditional on forecast cash flow, repayment capacity and cost of capital. Loan rate, term, repayment schedule, collateral capacity and incremental expansion cash flows are not supplied, so neither affordability nor inevitable default can be established. Retained profit is not necessarily retained available cash. Do not infer insolvency from one ratio.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-05-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-05-16: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-05-25','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',1,'finance','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.5','Paper 1 Section C/D-style broad essay (25)','To what extent should improving cash flow take priority over increasing profitability when a business is experiencing rapid growth?',NULL,25,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 25-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 selected knowledge: Distinguish internal/external finance, borrowing costs, liquidity and profitability measures. Extend accurately into the concepts named in the question, with appropriately precise business terminology.","AO2 plausible business contexts: Illustrate the argument using contrasting relevant businesses or reasoned hypothetical contexts; Paper 1 does not necessarily supply an individual case. Specific, coherent application is preferable to an unsubstantiated named brand.","AO3 developed analysis: Develop the distinction between profitable transactions and money available to pay immediate commitments. Analyse how rapid sales growth may consume working capital; compare benefits of pricing/margins with cash conversion and stock/credit control.","AO4 balanced judgement: Assess when liquidity is the binding survival condition and when profit improvement enables sustainable finance, accounting for business model and credit terms.","Valid alternatives: A well-argued counter-position can reach the highest level. Credit evidence-backed qualifications and alternative causal pathways; do not require a prewritten conclusion.","Level discriminator: High-band answers sustain two or more developed competing arguments, refer consistently to realistic context, weigh their relative significance and reach a comparative conclusion responsive to To what extent. Mid-band answers may analyse well but reach a weak/conditional-but-unexplained overall judgement; low-band answers list general advantages and disadvantages."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-05-25; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-05-25: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-06-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'hr','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.6','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','To what extent should Orchid Care Services increase spending on training to improve staff performance?','Orchid Care Services has 180 employees and annual labour turnover of 32%. Training costs £1,200 per recruit. Staff survey responses cite inconsistent rotas and a lack of career progression; customer satisfaction fell from 86% to 77%.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Explain training, turnover, motivation, retention and the difference between on-the-job and off-the-job development.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use 180 employees × 32% annual turnover = 57.6, about 58 leavers annually. 57.6 × £1,200 = £69,120 is an illustrative annual training cost only if each leaver is replaced and each recruit incurs that cost; the actual replacement rate and existing training budget are unknown. Use the rota concerns and customer satisfaction change 86% to 77% (9 percentage points).","AO3 developed causal analysis: Link higher competence to service quality and recruitment costs, but distinguish inadequate training from poor scheduling and progression. Explain time and opportunity costs plus the risk trained employees still leave.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Credit either targeted/phase-in training, or a justified rota/progression-first response, or a combination, if it weighs the case facts and trade-offs. The reported satisfaction decline does not establish that inadequate training caused it. Consider skills-gap and exit-reason evidence before committing substantial spending.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-06-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-06-16: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-06-25','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',1,'hr','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.6','Paper 1 Section C/D-style broad essay (25)','To what extent is financial reward the most effective way to improve employee motivation in a service business?',NULL,25,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 25-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 selected knowledge: Explain financial rewards such as wages, salaries, bonuses, commission, profit-sharing and performance-related pay, and their plausible effects on motivation, retention, fairness and effort. Credit accurately applied motivation theories (for example Herzberg, Vroom or equity theory), and contrast non-financial approaches such as responsibility, autonomy, job design or recognition. Training is only one optional non-financial comparison; do not mistake training types for the question''s main financial-reward demand.","AO2 plausible business contexts: Illustrate the argument using contrasting relevant businesses or reasoned hypothetical contexts; Paper 1 does not necessarily supply an individual case. Specific, coherent application is preferable to an unsubstantiated named brand.","AO3 developed analysis: Analyse pay as hygiene, fairness or retention influence without assuming it always changes motivation. Contrast job design, autonomy, recognition and progression using relevant motivation theories and the service context.","AO4 balanced judgement: Judge by worker needs, labour market, wage competitiveness and whether problems concern reward or working conditions.","Valid alternatives: A well-argued counter-position can reach the highest level. Credit evidence-backed qualifications and alternative causal pathways; do not require a prewritten conclusion.","Level discriminator: High-band answers sustain two or more developed competing arguments, refer consistently to realistic context, weigh their relative significance and reach a comparative conclusion responsive to To what extent. Mid-band answers may analyse well but reach a weak/conditional-but-unexplained overall judgement; low-band answers list general advantages and disadvantages."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-06-25; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-06-25: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-07-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'strategic-position','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.7','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Evaluate whether the financial indicators provide sufficient evidence for Kelton Mobility to approve its overseas investment.','Kelton Mobility plc has revenue £25m, operating profit £2m, capital employed £16m and non-current liabilities £9m. An overseas assembly proposal has a positive NPV of £900,000 but volatile demand and an unresolved supplier agreement.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Explain how profit margin, ROCE, borrowing/gearing risk and investment appraisal can inform but not settle decisions. A precise gearing measure must use a stated and supported formula; the supplied liabilities alone do not establish every definition''s debt and equity inputs.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Credit operating profit margin 8% (£2m/£25m), ROCE 12.5% (£2m/£16m), positive stated NPV £900,000 and sound use of the unresolved demand/supplier assumptions. Non-current liabilities are £9m relative to £16m capital employed (an arithmetic ratio of 56.25%); do not require calling 56.25% gearing without an explicit definition of borrowing, equity and denominator. Credit correctly stated alternative gearing conventions and the limits of the supplied data.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Analyse attractive returns against borrowing risk and forecast sensitivity. Consider market, competitor, operational and capability evidence beyond ratios.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Recommend further due diligence or a conditional approval tied to key assumptions, rather than declaring that a positive NPV guarantees success.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-07-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-07-16: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-08-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'strategic-direction','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.8','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Evaluate whether market development is a better strategic direction for Nova Homecare than product development.','Nova Homecare has a strong position in its UK specialist market. It could sell current services in two new regions for £400,000 or develop a subscription monitoring product for £1.1m. Research suggests demand in both options but the firm has limited digital expertise.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Explain market development and product development in Ansoff''s matrix and their distinct risk profiles.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use £400,000 regional entry, £1.1m new product and scarce digital capability rather than only generic matrix labels.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Link using an existing service to capability fit and geographical uncertainty. Compare subscription innovation''s recurring value with product-development execution and cost risks.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Recommend a choice justified by financial capacity, customer evidence and scarce capabilities, including circumstances favouring the alternative.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-08-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-08-16: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-08-25','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',1,'strategic-direction','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.8','Paper 1 Section C/D-style broad essay (25)','To what extent should a business prioritise differentiation rather than low cost when choosing how to compete?',NULL,25,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 25-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 selected knowledge: Explain differentiation through distinctive value, quality, design, service or brand position, versus low-cost/cost-leadership approaches based on an efficient cost base that may support competitive prices or margin. Distinguish a firm''s lower production cost from simply lowering selling prices. Ansoff''s growth matrix is not required unless a student independently uses a relevant, accurate growth strategy to support their argument.","AO2 plausible business contexts: Illustrate the argument using contrasting relevant businesses or reasoned hypothetical contexts; Paper 1 does not necessarily supply an individual case. Specific, coherent application is preferable to an unsubstantiated named brand.","AO3 developed analysis: Explain how differentiation can sustain brand loyalty and pricing power; analyse its costs and imitation risks. Contrast scale/purchasing, efficiency and low-cost strategies, including vulnerability to price competition and quality loss.","AO4 balanced judgement: Judge by the nature of the market, elasticity, firm resources and customer needs; state when hybrid trade-offs are sensible.","Valid alternatives: A well-argued counter-position can reach the highest level. Credit evidence-backed qualifications and alternative causal pathways; do not require a prewritten conclusion.","Level discriminator: High-band answers sustain two or more developed competing arguments, refer consistently to realistic context, weigh their relative significance and reach a comparative conclusion responsive to To what extent. Mid-band answers may analyse well but reach a weak/conditional-but-unexplained overall judgement; low-band answers list general advantages and disadvantages."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-08-25; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-08-25: MATERIAL / revised HOLD; new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-09-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'strategic-methods','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.9','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Evaluate whether acquiring the competitor is a better way for Briar Logistics to grow than opening depots organically.','Briar Logistics Ltd can acquire a regional competitor for £8m or open three depots organically for £5m. The acquisition adds 240 drivers but has incompatible IT systems and two disputed contracts. Managers want a national network within 18 months.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Distinguish external growth/acquisition from organic growth and describe potential synergy.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use £8m versus £5m, 240 drivers, IT incompatibility, disputed contracts and 18-month target.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Analyse faster capacity/customer access and synergies against debt, integration, culture and contract risk. Compare execution times, management stretch and predictability of greenfield depots.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Judge whether speed premium outweighs integration risk; identify synergy evidence and delivery milestones to verify.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-09-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-09-16: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-09-25','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',1,'strategic-methods','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.9','Paper 1 Section C/D-style broad essay (25)','To what extent is external growth the best strategic method for a business seeking rapid expansion?',NULL,25,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 25-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 selected knowledge: Distinguish external growth/acquisition from organic growth and describe potential synergy. Extend accurately into the concepts named in the question, with appropriately precise business terminology.","AO2 plausible business contexts: Illustrate the argument using contrasting relevant businesses or reasoned hypothetical contexts; Paper 1 does not necessarily supply an individual case. Specific, coherent application is preferable to an unsubstantiated named brand.","AO3 developed analysis: Develop economies of scale/scope and market share gains from acquisition; explain why synergy is not automatic. Contrast organic growth, alliances and international entry where context relevant, with financial/cultural control.","AO4 balanced judgement: Reach a conditional conclusion based on urgency, capital, capability and integration risk; avoid assuming external growth always beats organic.","Valid alternatives: A well-argued counter-position can reach the highest level. Credit evidence-backed qualifications and alternative causal pathways; do not require a prewritten conclusion.","Level discriminator: High-band answers sustain two or more developed competing arguments, refer consistently to realistic context, weigh their relative significance and reach a comparative conclusion responsive to To what extent. Mid-band answers may analyse well but reach a weak/conditional-but-unexplained overall judgement; low-band answers list general advantages and disadvantages."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-09-25; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-09-25: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('BUS-LF05-10-16','aqa:aqa-a-level:7132',2,'strategic-change','AQA 7132 v2.0: AQA 7132 v2.0 §3.10','Paper 2/3-style applied evaluation (16)','Assess whether Valen Foods should slow its digital change programme to consult employees more fully.','Valen Foods plc plans a digital inventory system across 12 warehouses. The pilot reduced stockouts by 18%, but 46% of surveyed staff worry about job security. Two site managers resist the rollout; suppliers need three months to adapt.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 accurate concepts: Explain strategic change, resistance, consultation, organisational culture and implementation planning.","AO2 contextual selection and interpretation: Use the 18% stockout improvement, 46% concern, 12 warehouses, supplier lead time and resistant site managers.","AO3 developed causal analysis: Explain how consultation improves implementation and reduces resistance while delays risk foregone inventory gains. Analyse communication, training and supplier readiness as alternatives to blanket delay.","AO4 evaluation and judgement: Recommend a phased consultation/implementation plan keyed to readiness metrics rather than ignoring staff or indefinitely postponing.","Valid alternatives: Reward a different recommendation if its assumptions and analysis are coherent with the facts; do not require the example''s suggested conclusion. Penalise unsupported inference and calculations not justified by the stimulus, not a different but defensible business choice.","Level discriminator: A 13–16-quality response integrates multiple actual facts from this specific case, develops at least two competing causal chains and prioritises a qualified conclusion. A 5–8-quality response mainly describes an idea and cites a fact without analysing its consequences. The exact band decision requires calibrated reviewed exemplars."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; BUS-LF05-10-16; AQA 7132 v2.0','Historical A3 43620992688 BUS-LF05-10-16: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required'),
('PSY-LF05-02-S1','aqa:aqa-a-level:7182',1,'memory','AQA 7182 v1.4: AQA A-level Psychology 7182, v1.4 (2027), §4.1.2, working-memory model (central executive, phonological loop, visuo-spatial sketchpad and episodic buffer). Paper 1 topic; proposed 16-mark-style training question. AO','Memory, contextual extended writing','Discuss how useful the working-memory model is for explaining the results of this investigation.','A researcher asks 24 sixth-form volunteers to remember six spoken digits while they complete a second task. In Task A, they track the changing positions of coloured shapes on a screen. In Task B, they repeat a sequence of spoken words. Average accuracy on the digit task is **76% in Task A** and **49% in Task B**. All participants completed both tasks, with Task A always first. The second tasks were not independently matched for difficulty. These figures are invented for the question; they are **not real research findings**.',16,'["REVIEW HOLD: original Revision 16-mark-style training; NOT an official AQA 2027 item or examiner grade. Needs independent question and guide acceptance before publication.","AO1 / knowledge: Explain the separate roles of the phonological loop (verbal/acoustic rehearsal), visuo-spatial sketchpad (visual/spatial processing), central executive (attentional control/coordination) and, where relevant, episodic buffer (integration). Distinguish their functions rather than presenting separate stores as a fixed proof of the model.","AO2 / actual data: The lower digit-task accuracy in Task B (49%) than Task A (76%) is consistent with greater interference when two concurrent tasks draw on verbal processing, compared with combining a verbal and a mainly visual/spatial task. The central executive may coordinate dual-task attention. The data do not isolate every component or measure capacity directly.","AO3 / challenge: Because Task A always came first and the secondary tasks were not matched for difficulty, order, fatigue and difficulty could also contribute. Question the limits of inferring specific working-memory mechanisms from just 24 sixth-form volunteers, whose recruitment method is not fully specified, and the group averages alone. Use dual-task evidence as a general model strength, while considering competing explanations, the central executive''s unclear boundaries and limitations of simplified laboratory tasks. Conclude cautiously.","Alternative credit: A well-reasoned argument about attentional demands, task interference or rehearsal is valid if it fits the prompt and does not claim the data alone establish a specific component. The episodic buffer may receive credit if appropriately connected, but it is not mandatory for every answer about this dual-task pattern.","What would weaken the answer: stating the numbers prove the working-memory model, confusing the phonological loop with visual processing, or claiming that the Task B accuracy difference was specifically caused by competing verbal processing when task difficulty and fixed task order are confounded. Comparing the task conditions is reasonable; attributing the difference to one component with certainty is not. Also avoid ignoring the investigation''s limitations.","Useful self-review questions (only after attempt): Did I cite both accuracy values? Did I explain why the task pairing matters? Did I account for the fixed order or unmatched difficulty? Did I use evaluation rather than describe only component definitions?","Qualitative differentiation, not a calibrated grade: A stronger answer links accurate mechanisms to both conditions, weighs the evidence against clear confounds and reaches a qualified judgement. A developing answer identifies the components but weakly connects the results. A limited answer merely lists memory stores or repeats percentages."]'::jsonb,'PR834 3b318fc53bce95195c8eef8b5771690c123f2a31; PSY-LF05-02-S1; AQA 7182 v1.4','Historical A3 43620992688 PSY-LF05-02-S1: NO MATERIAL (old exact bytes only); new A3/A4 required');
